Scene & Origin Lore
The movement materialized in 1978 at the Estación Biológica Alto Orinoco, where an unhinged collective of French-Venezuelan ethnobotanists and conservatory dropouts wired galvanic silver-chloride electrodes to the stems of wounded Monsteras and strangler figs. Disillusioned by cold academia and grieving the systemic felling of the river basin, they piped these chaotic microvolt currents into Moog modular synthesizers, overdriven electric pianos, and rented symphony players marooned in the jungle. The resulting subculture, known as Os Clorofílicos, wore hand-tailored safari linen jackets pinned with tarnished copper alligator clips and smudged their cheeks with oxidized green chlorophyll paste. Performance rituals took place at dusk in decaying botanical conservatories; audiences sat in utter silence with palms pressed against damp ground-wire copper plates, weeping collectively as virtuoso guitar solos locked into the dying electrical spasms of cut orchid vines while French horns echoed through the mist.

